  <Fragment slot="intro">
    qBittorrent runs on the Mac, and <a class="link" href="https://www.qbittorrent.org/download">qbittorrent.org</a> has its download. OrbTorrent runs the same engine inside a native Mac app: notarized by Apple, in your menu bar, and tied to your VPN if you like. Your qBittorrent downloads come over in one step.
  </Fragment>

  <h2>qBittorrent on a Mac today</h2>
  <p>qBittorrent is free, open source and has more features than most torrent apps. On the Mac, though, it has some rough edges:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Its own download page says the macOS version is “barely supported”,</strong> because the project has no active macOS developers. (<a href="https://www.qbittorrent.org/download">qbittorrent.org</a>)</li>
    <li><strong>It isn't notarized by Apple.</strong> The first time you open it, macOS says Apple could not verify it's free of malware, and you have to allow it in <strong>System Settings › Privacy &amp; Security</strong>. We checked version 5.2.3.</li>
    <li><strong>Its window is drawn by Qt,</strong> the same on Windows, Linux and the Mac, rather than with the Mac's own controls.</li>
    <li><strong>New versions of macOS can catch it out.</strong> After macOS 26 came out, some people found it <a href="https://github.com/qbittorrent/qBittorrent/discussions/23288">stopped downloading</a> until builds made for it arrived in 2026.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The same engine, in a Mac app</h2>
  <p>OrbTorrent bundles qBittorrent's engine, version {site.engine.qbittorrent} with libtorrent {site.engine.libtorrent}, built from qBittorrent's released source and checked against its signature. The engine runs in the background, and OrbTorrent is the app you see:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Native, and notarized.</strong> It's written in Swift and SwiftUI, with Liquid Glass, and opens without a warning.</li>
    <li><strong>In your menu bar.</strong> An orb shows how your downloads are doing, and a click shows speeds, downloads and their files.</li>
    <li><strong>Tied to your VPN, without the fiddling.</strong> qBittorrent lets you choose your VPN's network interface, but on a Mac <a href="https://safelyo.com/how-to-bind-qbittorrent-to-vpn/">its name can change</a> when the VPN reconnects or your Mac wakes, and downloads stall until you choose it again. OrbTorrent follows the VPN by itself, and stops if it drops.</li>
    <li><strong>Your VPN's forwarded port, kept in step,</strong> from VPNs that forward a port over NAT-PMP and from Private Internet Access.</li>
    <li><strong>Fake downloads held</strong> before they arrive, such as a “film” that's really a program.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What qBittorrent has that OrbTorrent doesn't</h2>
  <p>qBittorrent has a built-in torrent search, RSS feeds that download new episodes by themselves, a web interface for using it from another computer, and many more settings. OrbTorrent leaves out search and RSS on purpose, and remote access is planned: <a href={site.roadmap}>the roadmap</a> says why. If you rely on them, qBittorrent is the better app for you.</p>

  <h2>Bringing your downloads over</h2>
  <p>OrbTorrent <a href="/switch">imports qBittorrent's downloads</a> in one step, when you first open it or later from <strong>File › Import From</strong>. They keep their folders, file choices, names, labels and progress, and you choose which of qBittorrent's settings come over.</p>

  <Fragment slot="switch-title">The engine you trust, <span class="tone">made for your Mac.</span></Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="switch">
    <p>Download OrbTorrent and open it. It finds qBittorrent on your Mac and offers to bring your downloads and settings over, progress and all.</p>
  </Fragment>
